## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP with partitioning and additive segment costs; uses prefix sums to optimize the transition to O(nk).

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitioning into k segments
- Prefix sums to compute segment costs in O(1)
- State transition optimization with a running max and backpointers
- Reconstruction of cut points

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartitionScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  if (k <= 0 || k > n) return {score: 0, cuts: []};
  const diff = new Array(n-1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n-1; i++) diff[i] = Math.abs(A[i+1] - A[i]);
  const pref = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = pref[i-1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity, bestP = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const p = i - 1;
      const val = dp[p][t-1] - pref[p];
      if (val > best) { best = val; bestP = p; }
      dp[i][t] = pref[i-1] + best;
      take[i][t] = bestP;
    }
  }
  let cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = take[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = p;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

